{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790430316,"nonce":"2perOaBQIooBPcs3RZ+BpHlomps=","title":"bench_records: 600,000 small literal dicts cross the collector's second trigger with the hashed entry layout","message":"The entry layout (hash, key, value) costs a word per pair that the old [null, k, v, ...] list did not: a four-key literal row is 96 B of entry data where the old list was 72 B (80 B class). bench_records keeps 600,000 such rows alive; with about 250 B allocated per row the run now passes the collector's second trigger (32.8 MB before the first collection plus the 125 MB threshold after it) with about 591k rows built, and that collection marks about 149 MB in 55 to 68 ms of a 175 to 190 ms run (Mac, release, ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=1). At the old size the run ended about 10 MB short of the trigger. NUC wall medians, 5 rounds: 96152a35 167.7 ms, a0e96a87 211.9 ms (cf0738a4, which still had a control entry per small dict, was 212.1).\n\nLevers: record dicts as structs (P-dict-records), a hashless small form of the entry list, or the collector's trigger policy. Measurements from ZYNTAX_TRACE_GC=3 (per-class marked blocks) and ZYNTAX_TRACE_LOWER_PHASES=1.","files":null}]}